{
  "dom_id": "step-q313982",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A $15\\text{\u00a0ft}$ painter\u2019s plank leans against a wall. The bottom end slides horizontally away from the wall at a constant rate of $3\\text{\u00a0ft/s}.$ How fast is the top end sliding down the wall at the instant when it is $9\\text{\u00a0ft}$ above the ground? Assume the rate at which the top slides downward is **positive**.\n\nThe top end is sliding down at a speed of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/afdb53c3134f18add3b07400805c76a36f3f7a6b8265a3f32e9e0dfaf1dd36e9.png)\n\nIn the diagram, \n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the plank and the wall,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the plank and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the plank.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $y=9\\,\\text{ft}$ and $z=15\\,\\text{ft}.$ We can find $x$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{9}^{2} & ={15}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =225-81 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =144 \\\\ x & =12\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- The length of the plank remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(3)+2(9)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(15)(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{72}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the top end of the plank is sliding down at a speed of $4\\,\\text{ft/s}.$",
